---

Declare the extension entry point at compile time with `phpz.module`:

```zig
const extension = @import("extension_info");

comptime {
    phpz.functions(functions, .{});
    phpz.module(.{
        .name = extension.name,
        .version = extension.version,
        .classes = &.{CounterClass},
    });
}
```

The project defines `functions` and `CounterClass`. Names must match the stub and build configuration. The skeleton reads the extension name and version from `extension_info`.

# [Registration order]($heading.id('registration-order'))

PHP discovers functions through the module's function table. The phpz MINIT wrapper processes these entries in order:

1. The generated module symbol registration function, if present, registers module constants and other symbols.
2. The `.ini` settings.
3. The resource types in `.resources`, in array order.
4. The classes in `.classes`, in array order.
5. The `.observer` configuration, except for modules loaded temporarily with `dl()`.
6. `.module_startup_fn`.

Register each class only once through `.classes`. Resources are registered before classes so constructors and methods can create them. List parents and interfaces before dependent classes.

`.resources` accepts types returned by `phpz.zend.Resource.Typed(T, .{ .name = ..., .destroy = ... })`. Module startup assigns each resource type a process-local ID valid until module shutdown. Request-local resources are released with the request. See [PHP resources]($link.page('reference/types').ref('php-resources')).

```zig
const Value = struct { number: i64 };

fn destroy(value: *Value) void {
    phpz.heap.php_allocator.destroy(value);
}

const ValueResource = phpz.zend.Resource.Typed(Value, .{
    .name = "my_php_extension.value",
    .destroy = destroy,
});

comptime {
    phpz.module(.{
        .name = extension.name,
        .version = extension.version,
        .resources = &.{ValueResource},
        // Other settings, including .classes, omitted
    });
}
```

# [Lifecycle hooks]($heading.id('lifecycle-hooks'))

| Option | Phase | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `module_startup_fn` | MINIT | Initialize module state |
| `module_shutdown_fn` | MSHUTDOWN | Release module resources |
| `request_startup_fn` | RINIT | Reset state for this request |
| `request_shutdown_fn` | RSHUTDOWN | Clean up at request end |
| `info_fn` | MINFO | Display extension information |

The first four hooks have the signature `fn () anyerror!void`. `info_fn` takes a `*phpz.ModuleEntry`. Keep request-allocated PHP values within their request. For per-thread module state, see [Module globals]($link.page('advanced/globals')).

See [Lifetime boundaries]($link.page('reference/types/ownership').ref('lifetime-boundaries')) for cleanup responsibilities across calls, requests, objects and module globals.

# [Module entries for standalone hosts]($heading.id('module-entry'))

`phpz.module(options)` exports the module symbols for a shared or static extension according to its build configuration. `phpz.initModuleEntry(options)` instead returns a `ModuleEntry` for a host that supplies it during engine startup. It uses the same configuration and class registration rules and exports no dynamic loading entry point.

Keep the entry's storage alive through module shutdown. See [Additional modules and execution results]($link.page('advanced/sapi').ref('module-and-execution')) for passing it to the engine.
